# 2026-08-19 n8n Google OAuth credential breaks on production

## What I was trying to do
Reconnect my Google sheets and gmail credentials in n8n after I moved it from my localhost to a Hetzner server. 
## What broke
In the n8n credential screen the OAuth redirect URL was showing
```
http://localhost:5678/rest/oauth2-credential/callback
```
which was my laptop, and not the server.
Meaning that I tried to sign in on Google and it didn't work because Google was trying to go back to what was on my laptop and not the server. 
## What I thought was wrong
I thought it was because of an issue on the Google cloud console. It kinda was but it wasn't the main issue. 
## What was actually wrong
n8n didn't know its public URL because I didn't pass 
```
N8N_EDITOR_BASE_URL
```
and 
```
WEBHOOK_URL 
```
when I began the container on Docker. It just assumed localhost. 

Also, the client secret I pasted into n8n was wrong at first, so it showed me a 
```
invalid_client
```
error after the redirect URL got fixed.
## How I found it
Well the redirect URL in the credential screen was wrong, if n8n would've known its domain, it would've showed the right URL. When I saw the localhost there I knew that the env vars weren't there. 
The client secret issue showed up as a 
```
invalid_client
```
in the OAuth popup after the other stuff got fixed. 
## How I fixed it
I had to SSH into the server so that I could restart Docker with the right env vars. This is where it got a bit complicated, mainly because I didn't have my SSH passphrase written down anywhere. I was locked out. I ended up spending a lot of time going through the Hetzner rescue mode, which has a Finnish keyboard in the console which made typing passwords a lot harder, to the point of me looking for another solution. I ended up getting in by making a new SSH key on my Mac, and then I added it to the Hetzner security settings. Then I did another rescue so the key would load into the rescue system. Then I used Hetzner's "reset root password" so that I could go back to the server. I found the docker command in the bash history because there wasn't a docker compose file. So I restarted n8n with the env vars added.
```
docker stop n8n && docker rm n8n && docker run -d --name n8n -p 5678:5678 -e N8N_SECURE_COOKIE=false -e N8N_EDITOR_BASE_URL=https://n8n.esweetcakesbaking.com -e WEBHOOK_URL=https://n8n.esweetcakesbaking.com -v n8n_data:/home/node/.n8n docker.n8n.io/n8nio/n8n
```
When I saw that the redirect URL was right. I went back to the Google cloud console, and I added the production redirect URI, and copied the client secret. 
## Takeaway
There are basically 3 things I need to do right when setting up n8n. 
- Set both the
```
N8N_EDITOR_BASE_URL 
WEBHOOK_URL 
```
on the first docker run.
- Write down my SSH passphrase
- Save the client ID and secret somewhere 
